      "contentHash": "d0b7e72aa986fe0ce875e9a1ece20d67e0804d02ef0d98e5c83a1152c97f5d6f",
      "quote": "3.1 Modeling\nSimilar to prior works [Kerbl et al .2023; Zwicker et al .2001], we\nrepresent the scene with a set of 3D Gaussian primitives {G𝑘|𝑘=\n1,···,𝐾}. The geometry of each 3D Gaussian G𝑘is parameterized\nby an opacity 𝛼𝑘∈[0,1], center p𝑘∈R3×1, scale s∈R3×1, and\nrotation R∈R3×3parameterized by a quaternion:\nG𝑘(x)=𝛼𝑘𝑒−1\n2(x−p𝑘)𝑇𝚺−1\n𝑘(x−p𝑘)(1)\nwhere 𝚺𝑘∈R3×3is a covariance matrix defined as 𝚺𝑘=R𝑘s𝑘s𝑇\n𝑘R𝑇\n𝑘.\nRay Gaussian Intersection: Instead of projecting 3D Gaussians\nto 2D screen space and evaluating the Gaussian in 2D, we evaluate\nthe contribution of a Gaussian to a ray with explicit ray-Gaussian\nintersection [Gao et al .2023; Keselman and Hebert 2022].",
      "location": {
        "page": 3,
        "offset": 2022,
        "section": "3 METHOD / 3.1 Modeling",
        "sectionRole": "method",
        "sourcePassageStart": 0,
        "sourcePassageEnd": 662
      }
    },
    {
      "evidenceId": "ev_e3d7cc78021a29706c9154278704d4bf8d26892a76bb6e9b3e0c928ec207f110",
      "sourceWorkId": "arxiv:2404.10772",
      "sourceVersionId": "arxiv:2404.10772v1",
      "contentHash": "d0b7e72aa986fe0ce875e9a1ece20d67e0804d02ef0d98e5c83a1152c97f5d6f",
      "quote": " To circumvent these challenges, we intro-\nduce a novel method for extracting compact and adaptive meshes\nusing tetrahedral grids.\nTetrahedral Grids Generation: Our primary insight is that the\nposition and scale of 3D Gaussian primitives serve as reliable indica-\ntors for the presence of surfaces. To capitalize on this, we generate a\n3D bounding box at 3-sigma around each Gaussian primitive, where\nits center has highest opacity and its corners have smallest opacity.",
      "location": {
        "page": 4,
        "offset": 4052,
        "section": "3 METHOD / 3.4 Surface Extraction",
        "sectionRole": "method",
        "sourcePassageStart": 1053,
        "sourcePassageEnd": 1523
      }
    },
